gm-method (extends the float32 fractal-parity lesson): a CPU collider/veg that matches the GPU terrain HEIGHT (after float32 fround) can STILL warp HORIZONTALLY because a cube-sphere planet renderer maps its face-planar coordinate through faceWarp(p)=R*tan((p/R)*PI/4) before sampling the fractal. faceWarp is NOT identity at gameplay scale (~21% nonlinear compression: faceWarp(100m)=78.5m, faceWarp(250m)=196m). If the consumer's local->dir mapping is a LINEAR tangent map, it samples the fractal at a horizontally-stretched position vs the rendered surface -> metres of mismatch ('warping, bumps in different places'). WITNESS: compare CPU heightAt(localToDir) vs heightAt(faceWarp'd dir) at a few hundred metres - a multi-metre delta confirms it; and GPU sampleGroundM at the linear dir matches the faceWarp'd CPU height far better than the linear CPU height. FIX part 1: apply faceWarp(x),faceWarp(z) before the tangent offset in localToDir (and the curvature drop uses the warped offset) - closed most of the gap (2.5-3m -> ~0.15m at most dirs). FIX part 2 (the residual): a DIRECTION-DEPENDENT leftover (good at -x, bad at +x) means the consumer's tangent basis (east/north from an arbitrary cross(ref,up)) is ROTATED/MIRRORED vs the renderer's specific cube-FACE frame (faceFrame(iFace), col0=U/col1=V/col2=center) that anchorDir projects onto - align the consumer's east/north to that face's U/V. CAUTION: changing east/north rotates gameplay XZ. Two halves: faceWarp (magnitude) + cube-face-axis alignment (orientation). sampleGroundM is a noisy probe (stabilize per-dir); subtracting a float32 anchorHeight in a local-height calc confounds the absolute-vs-local comparison - compare like-for-like.